content.text:
Looking at the verifier, it normalizes the grid so red squares lie on the top row and blue squares on the left column. In that frame, the rule is: blue cells at column 0 are seeds; draw horizontal segments to the right, with segment boundaries at the (sorted) red columns plus 0 and the grid width; each successive segment shifts one row **down** (away from the red at top). Then transform back.

Applying to the given input: red at (3,0),(7,0) → left edge; blue at row 10, cols {2,4,6,8,10,13} → bottom edge. So in the original frame the lines run vertically, segments are split by rows (boundaries 0,3,7,11 with the "just before red" convention), and each segment shifts one column to the right.

Resulting segments:
- rows 8–10 at base cols {2,4,6,8,10,13}
- rows 4–7 at cols +1 → {3,5,7,9,11,14}
- rows 0–3 at cols +2 → {4,6,8,10,12,15}

with reds preserved at (3,0) and (7,0).

